<%# The headline and where the money came from. Local: `presenter` (DistributionPresenter).
    See the UI design spec §5.

    The breakdown is not decoration: "where did this number come from" is the first question a
    screen proposing to move $2,867 has to answer, and the three lines are the only place the
    swept money is visible as having come out of an envelope rather than out of thin air. %>
<div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded p-6">
  <% if presenter.available.positive? %>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">
      Distribute <%= number_to_currency(presenter.available) %>
    </h2>
  <% else %>
    <%# #available is deliberately NOT clamped at zero (Task 2): one account has no sibling
        overdraft to cancel against, so a floor would hide a real negative. "Distribute
        -$200.00" is not an instruction anyone can follow, so the headline says the true thing
        instead and the breakdown below still prints the figure. %>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-status-danger">Nothing to distribute</h2>
    <%# The red sentence is about an OVERDRAFT, so it fires on `negative?` and not on the
        headline's `!positive?`. An account sitting at exactly zero was reading "Checking is
        $0.00 in the red" — a debt of nothing, stated in the loudest colour the app has. An empty
        account is empty; it is not in trouble. %>
    <% if presenter.available.negative? %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-status-danger">
        <%= presenter.account.name %> is <%= number_to_currency(-presenter.available) %> in the red,
        sweeps included — nothing can be funded until money arrives.
      </p>
    <% else %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600">
        <%= presenter.account.name %> has no unallocated cash — nothing can be funded until money
        arrives.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <dl id="distribution-sources" class="mt-4 max-w-md space-y-1 text-sm">
    <%# Coloured on the same rule as Available and as the buffer line: a negative buffer is the
        one figure here that is a statement about being in trouble, and it was rendering in the
        same grey as the healthy ones while the two figures either side of it turned red. %>
    <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
      <dt class="text-gray-600">Buffer carried over</dt>
      <dd class="tabular-nums <%= presenter.buffer_carried.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>">
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.buffer_carried) %>
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
      <dt class="text-gray-600">Income this period</dt>
      <dd class="tabular-nums text-gray-900"><%= number_to_currency(presenter.income_this_period) %></dd>
    </div>
    <%# Money that left (or came back to) the buffer inside this period by some route other than
        a distribution: spending, and reallocations made by hand. Dropped when it is zero, which
        is the ordinary shape — a line reading "$0.00" invites the reader to look for a cause
        that is not there. %>
    <% unless presenter.moved_this_period.zero? %>
      <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-gray-600">Spent and moved this period</dt>
        <dd class="tabular-nums <%= presenter.moved_this_period.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>">
          <%= number_to_currency(presenter.moved_this_period) %>
        </dd>
      </div>
    <% end %>
    <%# Named pools, not a bare "swept back": the money left an envelope the user recognises,
        and with no sweep this line would read "Swept back from" followed by nothing. %>
    <% if presenter.sweeps.any? %>
      <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-gray-600">
          Swept back from <%= presenter.swept_pools.map(&:name).to_sentence %>
        </dt>
        <dd class="tabular-nums text-gray-900"><%= number_to_currency(presenter.total_swept) %></dd>
      </div>
    <% end %>
    <div class="flex justify-between gap-4 border-t border-gray-200 pt-1 font-semibold">
      <dt class="text-gray-900">Available</dt>
      <dd class="tabular-nums <%= presenter.available.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>">
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.available) %>
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <%# The all-clear density's ONE summary line. The waterfall does not render at all on this
      branch, so this sentence is the whole of what the screen has to say — hence the buffer
      figure in it, which the expanded branch prints as the waterfall's last row instead.
      `expanded?` and not `covered?`: the two densities are one decision, and keying the summary
      on a second predicate is how a screen ends up showing both or neither. %>
  <% unless presenter.expanded? %>
    <p id="distribution-summary" class="mt-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
      <% if presenter.lines.empty? %>
        Nothing is asking for money this period —
      <% else %>
        <%= pluralize(presenter.lines.size, "envelope") %> funded in full,
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.total_allocated) %> out —
      <% end %>
      <%= number_to_currency(presenter.leftover) %> stays in your buffer<%= buffer_target_clause(presenter) %>.
    </p>
    <%# THE WAY IN on a period that does not need the table (spec §5's "every line is editable",
        which the all-clear density otherwise has no line to offer). Nothing is remembered: the
        link carries the request, a fresh visit collapses again, so the density is still chosen by
        the data everywhere except where the user has explicitly asked otherwise. %>
    <p class="mt-3">
      <%= link_to "Show every envelope",
                  new_distribution_path(account_id: presenter.account.id, expand: 1),
                  class: "text-sm font-medium text-brand hover:text-brand-dark underline",
                  id: "distribution-expand" %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
</div>
